+++ b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0012-mtmd-omnivoice.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,5773 @@
+mtmd: speak with OmniVoice (k2-fsa/OmniVoice), text to speech by masked generative decoding
+
+OmniVoice is a Qwen3-0.6B read in both directions that fills the 8 codebooks
+of every audio frame in 32 steps, the most confident codes first, with
+classifier-free guidance; a DAC decoder (Higgs Audio v2's codec, 24 kHz, 25
+frames per second) turns the codes into audio. Until now it ran only in
+omnivoice.cpp, a program of its own on ggml.
+
+- libllama: the architecture `omnivoice` (src/models/omnivoice.cpp), Qwen3's
+  tensors with no KV cache and non-causal attention, like `dream`. A batch of
+  embedding rows stops at the hidden states: the vocabulary head (151,676
+  entries) would add a third to every decoding step for logits nobody reads.
+  A batch of tokens still gets logits from the embedding table, so the
+  caller's sampler has something to read (Kokoro's arrangement).
+- libmtmd: the projector type `omnivoice` (tools/mtmd/models/omnivoice.cpp).
+  GEN_CODE turns backbone states into the logits of the 8 codebooks (the
+  audio heads); GEN_WAV decodes codes: residual codebooks, then the DAC
+  decoder, op for op as in omnivoice.cpp but for the transposed convolutions
+  of odd stride, whose last sample is computed (ConvTranspose1d's
+  output_padding adds no zero) where omnivoice.cpp appends a zero.
+  mtmd_gen_audio_read_tensor() reads a quantized table (the audio code
+  embeddings).
+- mtmd-helper: the pipeline. A step is one batch of two sequences on a
+  backbone context the pipeline owns, so the caller's batch size never
+  bounds a sentence: the conditional one (style, text, the codes of a voice,
+  the target frames) and the unconditional one (the target frames alone). A
+  sequence only sees itself, which is how the reference masks: omnivoice.cpp
+  ADDS its mask to the attention scores (the behaviour of transformers'
+  "eager" attention on a boolean mask), so the unconditional target also
+  attends the padding there. Guidance, scoring, Gumbel noise and the
+  schedule are host code; a long text is cut into chunks, the first one is
+  the voice of the next ones. cfg_scale and flow_steps set the guidance and
+  the number of steps.
+- voice cloning: the mmproj also has an audio encoder, the projector type
+  `omnivoice_spkenc`. Its preprocessor lays a clip out at 24 kHz and at 16 kHz
+  (omnivoice.cpp's resampler); its graph is the codec's encoder - HuBERT and
+  a conv stack for the semantic half, a DAC encoder for the acoustic half, 8
+  residual codebooks - and returns one backbone row per frame, the sum of
+  the embeddings of its codes. The pipeline prepares the clip as the
+  reference does (level, silences), puts its rows before the target frames
+  and its transcript before the text, and gives the audio the clip's level.
+  mtmd_helper_get_capabilities() reports the model as taking a speaker
+  reference.
+- voice design: the text model's "tts" chat template puts a description of
+  the voice ("female, young adult, british accent") before the text, between
+  <|instruct_start|> and <|instruct_end|>; the pipeline holds it to the
+  vocabulary the model was trained on (omnivoice.cpp's table) and refuses
+  the rest.
+- vendor/omnivoice: omnivoice.cpp's host-side rules, unchanged (MIT): language
+  names, the duration estimate, the chunking of long texts, post-processing,
+  the resampler, the vocabulary of voice descriptions, and Philox4x32-10
+  (the generator of torch.rand on CUDA, the noise of the decoding).
+- MTMD_DEBUG_OMNIVOICE=<dir> dumps the prompt, each step and the codes of the
+  first chunk in omnivoice.cpp's dump format, to compare the two step by step
+  (the codes themselves cannot be compared: the decoding is chaotic under
+  rounding, a neutral change in omnivoice.cpp alone changes 85 % of them).
+
+The model files are omnivoice.cpp's two GGUFs repacked (names and metadata,
+no weight touched): a text model (-m) and an mmproj (-mm).
+
+Not yet: a chunk of a long text delivered as soon as it is decoded (the
+audio comes in one piece, as Kokoro's does).
